The key features of the graph are as follows:

- The domain is $(-\infty,\infty)$ and the range is $[1,\infty)$.
- It is an even function (the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis).
- It has no zeros.
- Its minimum value occurs at $x=0$.
- Its graph is decreasing over $(-\infty,0)$ and is increasing over $(0,\infty)$. Moreover,$\qquad$ $\cosh{x} 	o \infty$ as $x 	o \pm\infty$.
- It is the mean (average) of the functions $y=e^x$ and $y=e^{-x}$.

![](images/06/069613b68901d727d713bd50bbbe6a172348d41df12eabe9f0e735a2499d495a.png)

First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:

$$
\newcommand{\sech}{\mathop{\rm sech}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arsinh}{\mathop{\rm arsinh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcosh}{\mathop{\rm arcosh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\artanh}{\mathop{\rm artanh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arsech}{\mathop{\rm arsech}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcsch}{\mathop{\rm arcsch}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}
\tan{x} = \dfrac{\sin{x}}{\cos{x}}
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is

$$
\tanh{x} = \dfrac{\sinh{x}}{\cosh{x}}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u = \cosh{x}.$ Then,

$$
\dfrac{\textrm{d}u}{\textrm{d}x} = \sinh{x} \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \textrm{d}u = \sinh{x} \, \textrm d x.
$$

Now, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule $u = \cosh{x}.$

| $x$ | $0$ | $\ln 3$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $u$ | $1$ | $\dfrac{5}{3}$ |

Now, we evaluate the integral, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{1}^{5/3}\frac{1}{u}\,\text{d}u \\  & =\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\  & =\operatorname{ln}⁡\left(\frac{5}{3}\right)-\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\  & =\operatorname{ln}⁡\left(\frac{5}{3}\right)\end{aligned}
$$
